    axes: { harm: -1, pitch: 0, ornament: 0, meter: -1, density: -2, transmission: -2, improv: -1, soundTech: -2, intensity: -2, voice: 2, timbre: -1, percussion: -2, cyclicity: 2 },
    description: 'The Portuguese acalanto cradle-song tradition (also termed cantiga de berço or canção de embalar) continuous from medieval-and-early-modern Iberian cradle-song practice through 19th-century Romantic folksong collection by Almeida Garrett and into modern Lusophone domestic practice spanning Portugal, Cape Verde, Angola, Mozambique, and East Timor; defined by gentle ⁶⁄₈ or ¾ rocking meter, modal-major-with-occasional-minor-inflection melodic vocabulary, fado-adjacent emotional sweetness-and-melancholy register in Lisbon and Coimbra variants, Standard-Portuguese literary-text register coexisting with regional folksong survival. Distinguished from Brazilian Portuguese cantiga de ninar by Iberian fado-adjacent melodic-and-emotional inflection and the acalanto-name itself as more characteristic of Portuguese-than-Brazilian usage.',
    exemplars: ['"Dorme dorme meu menino" (traditional)', '"Vai-te embora ó papão"', '"Doce acalanto" (Beto Guedes 1981, modernized)', 'José Afonso — folk-revival cradle-song repertoire 1960s-1970s', 'Cape Verdean acalanto in Cesária Évora and broader morna lineage'],
