    description: 'The Bengali ghum-paranir gan ("sleep-bringing song") cradle-song tradition continuous from medieval Bengal domestic-vocal practice through Rabindra-sangeet appropriation (Tagore composed multiple cradle-songs and elevated the form into the Bangla art-song tradition) into modern Bangla-language domestic singing across West Bengal and Bangladesh; defined by raga-melodic-vocabulary inflecting the lullaby frame (Yaman and other tender-affect ragas common), free-meter or slow rocking ⁶⁄₈ phrasing, characteristic Bangla khol-and-harmonium accompaniment in domestic-classical context, Bangla literary-text register with frequent Rabindranath Tagore poetic-citation, intimate maternal-and-grandmother singing context. The Tagore "Ghum-paranir gan" repertoire from the Gitabitan canonical collection is foundational.',
    exemplars: ['"Ghum jay ghum jay" (traditional)', 'Rabindranath Tagore — multiple "Ghum-paranir gan" (Gitabitan)', '"Aaj amar maner kone" (Tagore-cradle)', '"Khoka ghumalo para juralo" (traditional)', '"Nidra Hara Nayan" (Tagore-cradle-adjacent)'],
