    description: 'The Yoruba ìrèmọjẹ (hunters\' and widows\' funeral dirge) tradition continuous from pre-colonial Yoruba ritual-practice through 19th-20th century continuous performance by Yoruba hunters\'-guild (egbé ọdẹ) and hunters\'-widows; particularly preserved in Ọ̀yọ́, Òṣun, Ọ̀ǹdó, Èkìtì Yoruba cultural zones in southwestern Nigeria. Defined by free-meter recitative Yoruba-vocal phrasing, Yoruba tonal-language constrained-melodic-vocabulary (the Yoruba three-tone system constrains the lament\'s melodic contour to follow speech-tone patterns), call-and-response group-participation with lead-singer and chorus, occasional dùndún talking-drum support, Yoruba-language text register with extensive ọrọ-ìrèmọjẹ (hunters\'-lament word) specialized vocabulary, performed during the dúró-ìrèmọjẹ vigil at the deceased hunter\'s compound. Distinguished from Yoruba praise-poetry oríkì by funeral-function and specialized ìrèmọjẹ-text-tradition.',
    exemplars: ['Documented Ọ̀yọ́ ìrèmọjẹ field-recordings (1960s-1970s ethnomusicology)', 'Akin Euba — Yoruba ritual-music documentation including ìrèmọjẹ', 'Ọ̀ǹdó ìrèmọjẹ widow\'s-lament tradition (Olusegun Stephens documentation)', 'Modern Yoruba funeral-traditional-revival recordings', 'Yoruba egbé ọdẹ hunters\'-guild ritual-songs cycle'],
