    description: 'The Ashkenazi-Jewish wedding-dance-music tradition centered on bulgar (Bulgarian-origin double-line dance form, ⁴⁄₄ moderately-fast tempo) and freylekhs ("joyful-dance," ⁴⁄₄ moderate-tempo line-dance); continuous from 19th-century Eastern-European-shtetl klezmer practice through post-1880s New-York Yiddish-American immigration era through the 1970s klezmer-revival. Defined by characteristic Freygish (Ahava Raba) modal vocabulary with augmented-second color, klezmer-clarinet-and-fiddle foreground virtuosic ornament (krekhts cry, dreydl turn, kneytsh wail), accordion-and-doibass-and-frame-drum dance-rhythm-section foundation, instrumental-dance-foreground with occasional call-and-response vocal interjection, characteristic mode-modulation across the dance-set, Yiddish wedding-functional context with badkhn (wedding-jester) verse-improvisation interludes. The Naftule Brandwein and Dave Tarras 1920s-1940s 78rpm recordings constitute the canonical pre-revival corpus.',
    exemplars: ['Naftule Brandwein — 1920s-1940s wedding klezmer 78rpm recordings', 'Dave Tarras — Yiddish wedding bulgar canonical recordings 1930s-1960s', 'The Klezmer Conservatory Band — 1980s-onward revival', 'The Klezmatics — modern Yiddish wedding repertoire', 'Naftule Brandwein "Der Heyser Bulgar"'],
