    description: 'The Aboriginal Australian hunting-and-songlines tradition continuous from at-least-65,000-year-continuous Indigenous-Australian cultural-practice through colonial-era continuous performance into modern Aboriginal-and-Torres-Strait-Islander continuous practice; the songlines (Tjukurpa "Dreaming" tracks in Pitjantjatjara-Yankunytjatjara terminology, or Wongar in Yolngu terminology) integrate hunting-game knowledge with cosmological-ancestral narrative across documented multi-thousand-kilometer song-cycles (the songlines are an Indigenous-Australian cartographic-and-knowledge-encoding system where specific land-features, water-sources, game-trails, and ritual-sites are encoded in song-sequence). Defined by didgeridoo-and-clapstick-and-voice arrangement, Aboriginal-language text register with regional-language variation, characteristic narrow-range narrative-recitative style.',
    exemplars: ['Yothu Yindi — modern Yolngu canonical', 'Wagilag Sisters Project canonical Yolngu Sister-songlines', 'Songlines as documented by Bruce Chatwin Songlines 1987', 'Geoffrey Gurrumul Yunupingu canonical modern Yolngu vocalist', 'Modern Aboriginal songlines-and-ceremony continuous-living tradition'],
    crossRefs: ['huntingSong.arctic', 'huntingSong.subSaharanAfrican', 'praiseSong.oceaniaPacific'],
