    description: 'The Apache-and-broader-Native-American hunting-medicine song tradition continuous from premodern Indigenous-North-American hunting-and-ritual practice through colonial-era continuous performance into modern Native-American continuous practice across Apache (Western Apache, Mescalero, Jicarilla, Chiricahua), Navajo (Diné), Lakota-Dakota-Nakota, and broader Indigenous-North-American cultures; the hunting-medicine songs invoke spiritual-power for successful game-pursuit (the canonical Apache "deer-medicine song" was historically performed before deer-hunting expeditions). Defined by powwow-drum-circle (or hand-drum in solo-ritual context) and voice arrangement, characteristic vocables-and-Indigenous-language text register, characteristic pentatonic-or-narrow-mode melodic vocabulary.',
    exemplars: ['Northern Cree — canonical powwow-drum-circle modern recordings', 'Black Lodge Singers — canonical powwow', 'Apache deer-medicine song continuous-living tradition', 'Lakota hunting-medicine song traditional', 'Frances Densmore early-20th-century Native-American song-documentation canonical scholarly'],
