  "canadian_folk": {"parent":"balladPoetry.angloSingerSong","axes":{"harm":0,"pitch":0,"ornament":0,"meter":0,"density":-1,"transmission":-1,"improv":0,"soundTech":-1,"intensity":-1,"voice":2,"timbre":0,"percussion":-1,"cyclicity":0},"description":"A broad geographic discovery category. This representative arrangement selects a sung-story branch with one acoustic guitar and a restrained plucked bass, keeping the lyric and melody clear. It does not reduce Canada’s distinct Indigenous, Francophone, maritime or instrumental traditions to the selected English-language songwriter example.","exemplars":["Gordon Lightfoot — Canadian Railroad Trilogy","Gordon Lightfoot — The Wreck of the Edmund Fitzgerald"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
  "canadian_singer_songwriter": {"parent":"balladPoetry.angloSingerSong","axes":{"harm":0,"pitch":0,"ornament":0,"meter":0,"density":-1,"transmission":-1,"improv":0,"soundTech":-1,"intensity":-1,"voice":2,"timbre":0,"percussion":-1,"cyclicity":0},"description":"This authorship-centered discovery category foregrounds the writer’s own sung interpretation. The default is an intimate voice-and-guitar performance, with no compulsory backing band; literary storytelling and melodic phrasing carry the arrangement. Piano-led and band-led branches remain part of the wider category.","exemplars":["Gordon Lightfoot — If You Could Read My Mind"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
  "british_folk": {"parent":"balladPoetry.celticBalladic","axes":{"harm":0,"pitch":0,"ornament":0,"meter":0,"density":-1,"transmission":-1,"improv":0,"soundTech":-1,"intensity":-1,"voice":2,"timbre":0,"percussion":-1,"cyclicity":0},"description":"A broad geographic umbrella with acoustic, vocal-harmony and electric revival branches. Here one singer narrates a traditional song over a responsive fingerpicked guitar, leaving room for irregular lyric phrasing. This is a representative small arrangement, not a prescription for Scottish, Welsh or every English repertoire.","exemplars":["Martin Carthy"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
  "black_americana": {"parent":"functionalSong.country.americana","axes":{"harm":0,"pitch":0,"ornament":0,"meter":1,"density":0,"transmission":-1,"improv":1,"soundTech":-1,"intensity":-1,"voice":2,"timbre":0,"percussion":-1,"cyclicity":0},"description":"A community and historical-perspective discovery category spanning more than one roots style. This authored arrangement selects sung lead and answering voices, open-back banjo, fiddle and acoustic guitar; it preserves the central string-band resources without prescribing a single sound to Black artists. Modern clean capture leaves ensemble interaction audible.","exemplars":["Our Native Daughters","Rhiannon Giddens","Amythyst Kiah","Leyla McCalla","Allison Russell"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":["balladPoetry.bluesNarrative"]},
  "danish_folk": {"parent":"improvOnFrame","axes":{"harm":0,"pitch":0,"ornament":1,"meter":1,"density":-1,"transmission":-1,"improv":1,"soundTech":-1,"intensity":-1,"voice":-2,"timbre":0,"percussion":-1,"cyclicity":0},"description":"The default is an instrumental trio with a clear fiddle line, responsive piano chords and plucked-guitar rhythm. It follows one contemporary Danish ensemble practice while allowing melody, accompaniment and improvisation to exchange roles. It does not impose accordion or a particular dance meter on every Danish folk recording.","exemplars":["Trio Mio","Kristine Heebøll","Peter Rosendal","Jens Ulvsand"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
  "croatian_folk": {"parent":"functionalSong","axes":{"harm":0,"pitch":0,"ornament":0,"meter":1,"density":1,"transmission":-1,"improv":0,"soundTech":-1,"intensity":-1,"voice":1,"timbre":1,"percussion":-1,"cyclicity":0},"description":"Group singing rests above interlocking plucked strings: bright prim melody, middle-register brač responses, bugarija chords and berda bass. Tremolo sustains the upper voices while chordal accents and alternating bass notes keep a clear rhythmic foundation.","exemplars":["LADO Orchestra","Linđo Folklore Ensemble"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
  "finnish_folk": {"parent":"functionalSong","axes":{"harm":0,"pitch":0,"ornament":1,"meter":1,"density":-1,"transmission":-1,"improv":0,"soundTech":-1,"intensity":-1,"voice":-2,"timbre":0,"percussion":-1,"cyclicity":1},"description":"The selected Kaustinen branch centers a fiddle section with harmonium and plucked bass, a documented configuration used by JPP. Articulated accents and danceable syncopation shape repeated tunes, with melody harmonization and authored additions possible. One fiddle card represents the section timbre. Other Finnish folk branches, including runosong and kantele practice, are not asserted to share this arrangement.","exemplars":["Kaustinen fiddle-playing community","Purppuripelimannit","JPP"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
