  "swedish_blues": {"parent":"functionalSong.bluesElectric","axes":{"harm":-1,"pitch":0,"ornament":1,"meter":0,"density":0,"transmission":0,"improv":1,"soundTech":1,"intensity":0,"voice":2,"timbre":0,"percussion":1,"cyclicity":1},"description":"Swedish blues includes Swedish-language writing and local interpretations of blues forms. Rolf Wikström’s singing, guitar and Stockholm band practice provide a documented singer-guitarist branch. This is a newly authored studio arrangement, not a reconstruction of the exemplar session.","exemplars":["Rolf Wikström — Hjärtslag (1977)"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
  "norwegian_blues": {"parent":"functionalSong.bluesElectric","axes":{"harm":-1,"pitch":0,"ornament":1,"meter":0,"density":0,"transmission":0,"improv":1,"soundTech":1,"intensity":1,"voice":2,"timbre":0,"percussion":1,"cyclicity":1},"description":"Norwegian blues includes club and festival ensembles performing blues and locally written blues-rock. V&BT documents a vocal-led quartet with guitar riffs, harmonic hooks and a bass-and-drums foundation. This is a newly authored studio arrangement, not a reconstruction of the exemplar session.","exemplars":["V&BT — Diamonds at the Rainbow’s End (Live in Norway, 2024)"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
  "arabic_hip_hop": {"parent":"mcRhythm.intlHipHop","axes":{"harm":0,"pitch":0,"ornament":1,"meter":2,"density":0,"transmission":1,"improv":1,"soundTech":2,"intensity":1,"voice":2,"timbre":1,"percussion":2,"cyclicity":2},"description":"An authored realization of DAM’s early Arabic-language rap production: an accompanied rhythmic verse over layered samples, sequenced hip-hop drums and an Arabic keyboard part. Suhel Nafar names an MPC 3000 and an Arabic keyboard in the group’s formative setup. The existing oriental-arranger preset supplies a practical keyboard realization without asserting its undocumented make or synthesis architecture. Sampled Arabic sounds are not mislabeled as live oud, qanun or percussion. The selected solo verse does not stand for every vocalist or passage on Ihda’.","exemplars":["DAM — Ihda’ / Dedication (2006); Suhel Nafar’s early MPC-and-keyboard practice"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
