  "drill_italiana": {"parent":"mcRhythm.drill","axes":{"harm":-1,"pitch":-1,"ornament":0,"meter":1,"density":0,"transmission":0,"improv":1,"soundTech":1,"intensity":1,"voice":2,"timbre":0,"percussion":2,"cyclicity":2},"description":"Rhythmic MC delivery and punctuating ad-libs sit over an808-style low-end drum bed and a repeated synth figure. This authored configuration selects the Chicago-leaning production branch discussed in the artist interview; it does not claim to encode every UK-drill portamento bass or syncopation in the broader scene. Exact machine and synth design are production choices.","exemplars":["Rondodasosa","NKO"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
  "finnish_hip_hop": {"parent":"mcRhythm.intlHipHop","axes":{"harm":1,"pitch":0,"ornament":1,"meter":2,"density":1,"transmission":1,"improv":1,"soundTech":2,"intensity":1,"voice":2,"timbre":1,"percussion":2,"cyclicity":2},"description":"A studio arrangement from the instrument palette of Don Johnson Big Band’s four-member formation: rap voice, keyboards, guitar, bass, drums, tenor saxophone and flute. Universal’s artist biography explicitly credits the drummer with guitar and bass as well, and one wind player with tenor sax/flute; these are overdubbable roles, not seven simultaneously performing band members. The generic workstation, single-coil guitar, electric-bass construction and kit materials are authored realizations. This Finnish scene example uses English-language rap, so Finnish is a scene marker rather than a promise about lyric language.","exemplars":["Don Johnson Big Band — Breaking Daylight (2003), label-documented studio instrument palette"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
  "german_jazz_rap": {"parent":"mcRhythm.intlHipHop","axes":{"harm":1,"pitch":0,"ornament":1,"meter":2,"density":1,"transmission":1,"improv":2,"soundTech":1,"intensity":1,"voice":2,"timbre":1,"percussion":2,"cyclicity":2},"description":"A German-language MC front line with played guitar, keyboards, bass and drum kit, saxophone/trumpet colors and DJ scratches. Jazzkantine’s own 2019 press sheet explicitly lists this nine-person touring band; two rappers share the aggregate voice role. It also describes the album’s jazz, soul and reggae inflections. The model follows that credited core rather than importing every album guest or the separate NDR big-band collaboration. Single-coil guitar, generic workstation, tenor saxophone and precise construction/technique choices are authored representative realizations, not asserted as an equipment inventory.","exemplars":["Jazzkantine — Mit Pauken und Trompeten (2019), documented core album-tour formation"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
  "german_underground_rap": {"parent":"mcRhythm.intlHipHop","axes":{"harm":0,"pitch":-1,"ornament":0,"meter":1,"density":1,"transmission":1,"improv":1,"soundTech":1,"intensity":1,"voice":2,"timbre":0,"percussion":2,"cyclicity":2},"description":"An independent German MC-and-producer branch based on jazz/funk record fragments, beat construction and turntable manipulation. Retrogott describes underground as an artistic stance rather than a sales threshold; the model follows the duo’s sample-led practice, not every regional underground scene. Exact MPC architecture, sample resolution, swing, microphone and room are authored defaults. It does not inherit a live jazz orchestra from the sampled records.","exemplars":["Retrogott & Hulk Hodn — Fresh und umbenannt (2013), sample-led MC/DJ branch"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
  "ghanaian_hip_hop": {"parent":"mcRhythm.afroDiasporic","axes":{"harm":0,"pitch":0,"ornament":0,"meter":2,"density":0,"transmission":1,"improv":1,"soundTech":2,"intensity":1,"voice":2,"timbre":1,"percussion":2,"cyclicity":2},"description":"Rap voice over digitally programmed drums, bass and melodic layers in a representative arrangement grounded in Magnom’s account of producing Sarkodie’s Illuminati. The producer describes his Fruity Loops workflow, beat-making from drum, bass or melody ideas and studio vocal recording. Sample-drum sequencing plus virtual-analog synthesis is the authored sound-producing implementation, not a claim that he owned the named hardware or used one specific synth on the track. A software DAW is not added as a separate acoustic instrument.","exemplars":["Sarkodie — Illuminati, produced by Magnom (recording described as 2012)"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
  "hardcore_hip_hop": {"parent":"mcRhythm.usHipHop.eastCoast","axes":{"harm":-1,"pitch":0,"ornament":0,"meter":2,"density":2,"transmission":1,"improv":1,"soundTech":2,"intensity":2,"voice":2,"timbre":2,"percussion":2,"cyclicity":2},"description":"A forceful MC delivery over a dense collage of rhythm fragments, loops and manipulated records. Chuck D and Hank Shocklee describe the construction of It Takes a Nation of Millions and the turntable-to-sampler process on Don’t Believe the Hype. The SP-1200-family twelve-bit setup is a documented period production tool and an authored representative realization, not a claim that every sound in the exemplar passed through one machine. Acoustic sources inside samples are not recreated as a live funk band, and the later Anthrax guitar crossover is not inserted here.","exemplars":["Public Enemy — Don’t Believe the Hype, It Takes a Nation of Millions to Hold Us Back (1988)"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
  "hip_hop_cubano": {"parent":"mcRhythm.intlHipHop","axes":{"harm":0,"pitch":0,"ornament":1,"meter":2,"density":1,"transmission":1,"improv":1,"soundTech":2,"intensity":1,"voice":2,"timbre":1,"percussion":2,"cyclicity":2},"description":"Rap and melodic vocal refrains arranged with looped Cuban-record material and hip-hop percussion. Yotuel explains that Orishas substituted Cuban source recordings for the US funk samples they had previously followed; Roldán and Ruzzo describe joining son/clave and DJ beats. A phrase sampler carries both sampled acoustic timbres and rhythmic layers here. The late-1990s sixteen-bit sampler architecture is an authored realization, not a verified machine credit. A sampled trumpet or hand drum does not require adding a live salsa band.","exemplars":["Orishas — A lo cubano, selected sample-built branch (1999)"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
  "hip_hop_galsen": {"parent":"mcRhythm.afroDiasporic","axes":{"harm":0,"pitch":0,"ornament":1,"meter":2,"density":1,"transmission":1,"improv":1,"soundTech":1,"intensity":1,"voice":2,"timbre":1,"percussion":2,"cyclicity":2},"description":"Wolof-centered rap and melodic singing with played bass, kit, guitar and keyboards in the selected Daara J Family touring arrangement. A primary 2020 program credits Faada Freddy and Ndongo D, a supporting voice and the four instrumental roles. The guitar pickup type, workstation architecture and wood/metal/technique settings are authored realizations of those credits. This later ensemble is distinct from the group’s early voice-and-drum-machine beginnings; no kora, sabar or djembe is inferred from Senegalese geography.","exemplars":["Daara J Family — Yamatélé (2020), documented album-tour formation"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
  "hip_hop_quebecois": {"parent":"mcRhythm.intlHipHop","axes":{"harm":0,"pitch":0,"ornament":1,"meter":2,"density":1,"transmission":1,"improv":1,"soundTech":2,"intensity":1,"voice":2,"timbre":1,"percussion":2,"cyclicity":2},"description":"An interlocking French-language rap front line, sampled spoken material and rhythmic layers, keyboard colors and a played trumpet motif. Research on Langage-toi distinguishes its credited trumpet from sampled material, and reports Chafiik’s broader mix of samples, MIDI instruments and studio recordings. The sampler carries the rhythm/bass and ambiguous guitar-like texture in this authored realization; no separate live guitar or acoustic kit is asserted without a performer credit. Workstation and sampler models, brass construction and recording chain are representative defaults rather than documented session gear.","exemplars":["Loco Locass — Langage-toi, Manifestif (2000)"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
