  "emo_trap_en_espanol": {"parent":"mcRhythm.intlHipHop","axes":{"harm":0,"pitch":0,"ornament":1,"meter":2,"density":1,"transmission":1,"improv":1,"soundTech":2,"intensity":0,"voice":2,"timbre":1,"percussion":1,"cyclicity":2},"description":"Melodic rap and sung exchanges over a layered sampled backing. The distributor credits Rojuu’s rap and Carzé’s sampler on Cuando Sonríes, alongside Saramalacara and Evar. Multiple vocal contributors aggregate within the voice role. The modern sampler patch, intimate delivery, electronic pitch treatment and layered production are authored defaults rather than exact hardware or plugin claims. Rojuu’s later interview supports treating vocal processing as an artistic choice, but his later live-band lineup is not backdated into this recording. The label describes a transnational discovery scene, not a single uniform Spanish-language sound.","exemplars":["Rojuu, Saramalacara, Carzé & Evar — Cuando Sonríes (2020; included in Grandes Hits before 18)"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
  "florida_rap": {"parent":"mcRhythm.usHipHop.southern","axes":{"harm":-1,"pitch":0,"ornament":1,"meter":2,"density":2,"transmission":1,"improv":1,"soundTech":2,"intensity":2,"voice":2,"timbre":2,"percussion":2,"cyclicity":1},"description":"Forceful rap and elastic vocal phrasing over rapidly recombined sampled textures and programmed beats. Curry and Kenny Beats describe UNLOCKED as a collaborative departure toward boom-bap-inspired collage; Kenny specifies sourced audio rather than sampled commercial records. This is his account of production, not an independent license ruling on those sounds, and no audio is reused here. A modern high-resolution sampler with tight fragment editing is the authored realization; it does not invent live brass, strings or guitar players from the source timbres.","exemplars":["Denzel Curry × Kenny Beats — UNLOCKED (2020)"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
  "german_alternative_rap": {"parent":"mcRhythm.intlHipHop","axes":{"harm":1,"pitch":0,"ornament":1,"meter":2,"density":1,"transmission":1,"improv":1,"soundTech":2,"intensity":0,"voice":2,"timbre":1,"percussion":1,"cyclicity":1},"description":"Rap and song delivery with programmed/recorded fragments, piano and guitar in a representative hybrid studio arrangement. In his Grau-era Q&A, Tua describes writing from an idea, recording his own voice, working in Cubase/Reason and playing real piano or guitar. A later producer interview explains turning recorded everyday sounds into drum hits. The grand piano and single-coil guitar are authored realizations of the named played roles; the sampler includes rhythmic source recordings rather than adding a door or lighter as separate instruments. No claim is made that this is the complete equipment or track list for Grau.","exemplars":["Tua — Grau-era studio practice (2009), with documented 2011 sound-design continuity"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
  "german_trap": {"parent":"mcRhythm.intlHipHop","axes":{"harm":0,"pitch":0,"ornament":0,"meter":2,"density":1,"transmission":1,"improv":1,"soundTech":2,"intensity":1,"voice":2,"timbre":1,"percussion":2,"cyclicity":2},"description":"Rhythmic rap with processed melodic inflections over layered sample material and sequenced percussion. Sonus030 describes combining two unrelated samples for Balenciaga, with AT Beatz supplying breaks and bridges. Ufo361’s contemporary interview identifies software-based 808 production and explains his move toward tuned melodic delivery. The sampler and drum-machine roles are functional catalog representations of computer production, not a claim that an MPC or original TR-808 was used. Exact resolution, retune speed and drum programming defaults are authored.","exemplars":["Ufo361 — Balenciaga (2018), produced by Sonus030 and AT Beatz"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
  "hungarian_hip_hop": {"parent":"mcRhythm.intlHipHop","axes":{"harm":1,"pitch":0,"ornament":1,"meter":2,"density":2,"transmission":1,"improv":1,"soundTech":1,"intensity":1,"voice":2,"timbre":1,"percussion":2,"cyclicity":2},"description":"Rap and sung refrains with DJ, keyboards, guitar, electric bass, violin, drum kit and trumpet. Müpa’s own 2016 concert billing names each played role and an additional violin guest; the violin and voice entries aggregate multiple performers rather than omit them. This concert-band model retains the ensemble instead of treating all accompaniment as sampled. Keyboard preset, guitar pickup design, bass setup, violin materials, recording room and chain are authored representative choices. The licensed 2013 festival image illustrates the band in an earlier lineup and does not establish the 2016 bass-guitar credit.","exemplars":["Punnany Massif — Müpa concert-band presentation (29 December 2016)"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
