  "chinese_black_metal": {"parent":"distortedRock.metal","axes":{"harm":1,"pitch":0,"ornament":1,"meter":0,"density":1,"transmission":0,"improv":0,"soundTech":1,"intensity":2,"voice":1,"timbre":2,"percussion":1,"cyclicity":1},"description":"China’s black-metal scene includes multiple approaches. This selected Zuriaake touring branch joins atmospheric black metal with Mandarin vocal delivery, melodic lead-guitar phrasing and sampled bell, natural and traditional-instrument textures. Contemporary firsthand concert reporting documents electric guitars, bass and an acoustic drummer alongside the sample layer. The model represents these five sound-source families; it does not turn sampled timbres into invented live traditional-instrument players.","exemplars":["Zuriaake — 2018–2019 touring branch"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
  "christian_deathcore": {"parent":"distortedRock.metal","axes":{"harm":0,"pitch":0,"ornament":0,"meter":1,"density":2,"transmission":0,"improv":0,"soundTech":1,"intensity":2,"voice":1,"timbre":1,"percussion":2,"cyclicity":1},"description":"Christian deathcore combines deathcore musical practice with explicitly Christian themes. This selected Impending Doom branch follows The Serpent Servant-era ensemble: distorted electric-guitar parts, bass, acoustic drums and harsh lead voice, with blast-beat passages alternating with half-time breakdowns. Brook Reeves directly establishes the band’s Christian intent and explains its development from earlier grinding death metal toward more spacious, groove-based writing. The authored model focuses on the later deathcore contrast rather than conflating all Christian extreme metal.","exemplars":["Impending Doom — The Serpent Servant (2009)"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
  "christian_metalcore": {"parent":"distortedRock.metal","axes":{"harm":0,"pitch":0,"ornament":0,"meter":1,"density":1,"transmission":0,"improv":0,"soundTech":1,"intensity":2,"voice":1,"timbre":1,"percussion":2,"cyclicity":1},"description":"Christian metalcore is a metal/hardcore field with explicitly Christian lyrical and community commitments. For Today supplies this selected early-2010s branch: distorted, rhythmically articulated guitars, bass, drum kit and a forceful vocal lead. Its singer directly describes the band’s evangelistic purpose, and its label documents the hardcore/metal combination. The authored core emphasizes chugging breakdowns and shouted delivery; clean vocal responses, acoustic versions and record-specific electronic accents remain alternate arrangements.","exemplars":["For Today — early-2010s electric-band branch"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
  "czsk_black_metal": {"parent":"distortedRock.metal","axes":{"harm":1,"pitch":0,"ornament":1,"meter":0,"density":1,"transmission":0,"improv":0,"soundTech":1,"intensity":1,"voice":1,"timbre":1,"percussion":1,"cyclicity":1},"description":"CZSK black metal groups Czech and Slovak black-metal scenes rather than prescribing one national sound. This selected Czech Root branch uses heavy electric-guitar riffs, bass, acoustic drums and Big Boss’s clear, dramatic singing. Root’s musicians describe composing from lyrics and transposing preliminary keyboard sketches into guitar riffs, then recording the prepared parts as a band. The model follows that guitar-centered arrangement; it does not add a keyboard player merely because keyboards were used to sketch songs.","exemplars":["Root — guitar-centered Kärgeräs / later dark-metal branch"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
  "dub_metal": {"parent":"distortedRock.metal","axes":{"harm":0,"pitch":0,"ornament":1,"meter":1,"density":1,"transmission":0,"improv":0,"soundTech":1,"intensity":1,"voice":1,"timbre":1,"percussion":2,"cyclicity":2},"description":"Dub metal is a crossover label for heavy riff-based music that incorporates dub/reggae bass, vocal and electronic-production practices. This selected Skindred branch retains guitar, electric bass, acoustic drums, a toasting-led vocal part and sampler/electronics. Dan Pugsley directly describes bringing Dan Sturgess into the live ensemble to handle samples and spontaneous transitions. Skindred’s broader repertoire also includes melodic singing and other dance-music influences; the label does not describe every song.","exemplars":["Skindred — dub/ragga-metal touring and recording branch"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
  "electronic_djent": {"parent":"distortedRock.metal","axes":{"harm":0,"pitch":0,"ornament":1,"meter":2,"density":1,"transmission":0,"improv":0,"soundTech":2,"intensity":2,"voice":-2,"timbre":2,"percussion":1,"cyclicity":1},"description":"Electronic djent is used here as the source discovery-cluster label, whose roster includes The Algorithm. Rémi Gallego explicitly resists the djent label, describing an experimental electronic/progressive-metal crossover instead. This selected mid-2010s branch combines extended-range electric guitar, live acoustic drums and software FM/wavetable synthesis. His interviews establish computer-based arrangement, MIDI control and an increasing live guitar role. The authored instrumental model preserves those sources without inventing an electric-bass player or lead singer.","exemplars":["The Algorithm — Brute Force-era electronic/progressive-metal branch"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
  "epic_black_metal": {"parent":"distortedRock.metal","axes":{"harm":1,"pitch":0,"ornament":1,"meter":0,"density":0,"transmission":0,"improv":0,"soundTech":1,"intensity":1,"voice":1,"timbre":2,"percussion":1,"cyclicity":1},"description":"Epic black metal is retained as the source discovery-cluster label, with Zuriaake explicitly present in its artist roster. This selected long-form atmospheric branch emphasizes melodic electric-guitar lines, bass, drums, harsh voice and sampled environmental/traditional timbres. The official Gu Yan EP contains extended pieces rather than a short verse-chorus template; contemporary concert reporting establishes the real band and sample roles. Epic does not impose Tolkien themes, a European folk ensemble or symphonic instrumentation on every member.","exemplars":["Zuriaake — Gu Yan long-form atmospheric branch"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
