  "old_school_hip_hop": {"parent":"mcRhythm.usHipHop.eastCoast","axes":{"harm":0,"pitch":0,"ornament":0,"meter":2,"density":1,"transmission":2,"improv":2,"soundTech":1,"intensity":1,"voice":0,"timbre":1,"percussion":2,"cyclicity":2},"description":"A rhythmically cut collage performed from records on multiple decks, preserving break passages, vocal fragments and scratch gestures as one live turntable performance. Flash describes recording Wheels of Steel with three decks rather than assembling digital-sampler tracks. One turntable catalog role aggregates all three decks; prerecorded voices and band sounds are not falsely credited as new live singers or instrumentalists. The analog capture chain and optional room-microphone color are authored reconstruction choices, not the historical session’s equipment inventory. No source audio is reproduced or licensed by this packet.","exemplars":["Grandmaster Flash — The Adventures of Grandmaster Flash on the Wheels of Steel (1981)"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
  "old_school_rap_francais": {"parent":"mcRhythm.intlHipHop","axes":{"harm":0,"pitch":0,"ornament":0,"meter":2,"density":0,"transmission":1,"improv":1,"soundTech":1,"intensity":1,"voice":2,"timbre":0,"percussion":2,"cyclicity":2},"description":"Conversational rhythmic verses over looped funk-source material and sequenced beat layers. Akhenaton’s contemporary interview confirms the Benson sample and explains the song’s tribute to his generation’s funk parties; the record label’s history confirms the sampled guitar phrase. Guitar, bass and other timbres inside the record source remain within the sampler role rather than being mislabeled as live players. A twelve-bit sampler and lightly swung loop construction are authored period-informed choices; no exact machine claim is made for the session.","exemplars":["IAM — Je danse le mia, Ombre est lumière era (1993–94)"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
  "palestinian_hip_hop": {"parent":"mcRhythm.intlHipHop","axes":{"harm":0,"pitch":0,"ornament":1,"meter":2,"density":1,"transmission":1,"improv":1,"soundTech":2,"intensity":1,"voice":2,"timbre":1,"percussion":2,"cyclicity":2},"description":"Collective rap verses and refrain responses over Arabic-source samples, programmed hip-hop drums and an Arabic keyboard. DAM’s members describe the early MPC-and-keyboard setup and the process of expanding solo demos into a three-MC album. The ensemble aggregates the rappers in one voice role; call-and-response is an authored arrangement of that collective delivery. The keyboard’s existing Korg arranger preset is a representative realization, not a sourced make/model. No sampled Arabic timbre is promoted to a live oud, qanun or percussion credit.","exemplars":["DAM — Ihda’ / Dedication (2006), formative collective production practice"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
  "finnish_metal": {"parent":"distortedRock.metal","axes":{"harm":1,"pitch":0,"ornament":1,"meter":0,"density":1,"transmission":0,"improv":0,"soundTech":1,"intensity":1,"voice":1,"timbre":1,"percussion":1,"cyclicity":1},"description":"Finnish metal is a broad geographic field. This selected Amorphis Circle-era piano-centered branch combines electric guitar, five-string electric bass, acoustic drums and voice with grand piano. The musicians distinguish simple melodic writing from merely displaying virtuosity. The recipe is an authored electric-band/piano arrangement, not a claim that one ensemble or the band’s folk influences define all Finnish metal.","exemplars":["Amorphis — Circle-era piano-centered band branch"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
  "finnish_progressive_metal": {"parent":"distortedRock.metal","axes":{"harm":1,"pitch":0,"ornament":2,"meter":1,"density":1,"transmission":0,"improv":0,"soundTech":1,"intensity":1,"voice":1,"timbre":2,"percussion":1,"cyclicity":1},"description":"Finnish progressive metal includes keyboard-rich rock/metal combinations. This selected Amorphis Circle-era arrangement expands the electric guitar, five-string bass, drum and vocal core with grand piano, Hammond-style tonewheel organ and monophonic synth lead. The primary studio interview documents these distinct keyboard colors; they are not compressed into an invented generic orchestral patch. This model emphasizes the band’s contrasting keyboard and guitar parts rather than requiring odd meter in every passage.","exemplars":["Amorphis — Circle-era keyboard-rich progressive branch"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
  "german_metal": {"parent":"distortedRock.metal","axes":{"harm":0,"pitch":0,"ornament":1,"meter":0,"density":1,"transmission":0,"improv":0,"soundTech":2,"intensity":2,"voice":1,"timbre":1,"percussion":2,"cyclicity":2},"description":"Germany’s metal field contains many distinct scenes. This selected Rammstein industrial-metal branch combines palm-muted electric guitars, electric bass, acoustic drums, emphatic vocal phrasing and sampled keyboard textures. The musicians’ accounts distinguish live keyboard melodies and pads from computer-triggered sequences, and conventional amplification from direct guitar processing. This authored band-core model retains the electronic keyboard role; theatrical stage effects are not musical instruments.","exemplars":["Rammstein — riff-and-keyboard industrial-metal band core"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
  "german_power_metal": {"parent":"distortedRock.metal","axes":{"harm":1,"pitch":0,"ornament":1,"meter":0,"density":1,"transmission":0,"improv":0,"soundTech":1,"intensity":2,"voice":1,"timbre":1,"percussion":2,"cyclicity":1},"description":"Germany’s power-metal scene is represented here by Blind Guardian’s guitar-led band core: harmonized electric-guitar leads, electric bass, drums and layered melodic voice. The artist’s official history establishes its Krefeld origin and evolution from speed metal, while its 1995 album notes explicitly credit Hansi Kürsch’s bass role. This selected recording model emphasizes electric-band passages and choral vocal layering rather than claiming that all German power metal requires a full orchestra.","exemplars":["Blind Guardian — guitar-led melodic/speed-metal band branch"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
