  "swedish_reggae": {"parent":"bassSystem.reggaeDiaspora","axes":{"harm":0,"pitch":0,"ornament":0,"meter":0,"density":1,"transmission":1,"improv":0,"soundTech":1,"intensity":0,"voice":0,"timbre":0,"percussion":1,"cyclicity":2},"description":"Malmö singer’s Swedish-language roots-reggae release credits Felisia Westberg and Isak Strand with backing voices; flute, guitar, bass, keyboard, sax, trombone, trumpet and drums are explicit. Exact production, hardware, tuning baseline, technique defaults and axes are authored modern choices.","exemplars":["Efia Abu & Drömbandet — Kärlek & Verkan"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
  "swiss_reggae": {"parent":"bassSystem.reggaeDiaspora","axes":{"harm":0,"pitch":0,"ornament":0,"meter":0,"density":1,"transmission":1,"improv":0,"soundTech":1,"intensity":0,"voice":0,"timbre":0,"percussion":1,"cyclicity":2},"description":"Lead and backing singing sit over bass, drums and offbeat guitar. Piano and organ add chordal weight and rhythmic replies, while trombone and other horns supply melodic colour. The interaction of bass movement, drum accents and clipped accompaniment creates the groove. Swiss bands and local recording communities adapt this musical vocabulary through their own songs and arrangements.","exemplars":["Los Billtones — Back In Time"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
  "uk_reggae": {"parent":"bassSystem.reggaeDiaspora","axes":{"harm":0,"pitch":0,"ornament":0,"meter":0,"density":1,"transmission":1,"improv":0,"soundTech":1,"intensity":0,"voice":0,"timbre":0,"percussion":1,"cyclicity":2},"description":"Bass and drums establish the groove, with clipped guitar and keyboard chords marking offbeats around the singer. Horn parts and backing voices enrich some arrangements. Lyrics encompass community life, social criticism and personal themes; instrumental dub versions rearrange the same musical materials through mixing. The interplay between band performance, studio production and sound-system listening is central to the British scene.","exemplars":["Talisman — Don’t Play with Fyah, Dennison Joseph title-track branch"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
  "trinidadian_reggae": {"parent":"bassSystem.reggaeDiaspora","axes":{"harm":0,"pitch":0,"ornament":0,"meter":0,"density":1,"transmission":1,"improv":0,"soundTech":1,"intensity":0,"voice":0,"timbre":0,"percussion":1,"cyclicity":2},"description":"Lead singing and vocal responses sit over bass, drums and offbeat guitar or keyboard accompaniment. A firm low end and rhythmic space give the words room, while instrumental replies connect phrases. Diaspora artists such as John Blood carry Trinidadian storytelling into band and studio reggae, linking regional identity with an international musical practice.","exemplars":["John Blood — The Journey, track 3 ensemble branch"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
  "uk_dub": {"parent":"bassSystem.dubDescendants","axes":{"harm":0,"pitch":0,"ornament":0,"meter":0,"density":1,"transmission":1,"improv":0,"soundTech":1,"intensity":0,"voice":-2,"timbre":0,"percussion":1,"cyclicity":2},"description":"Artist release documents dub production in Manchester and credits Birchall and Breadwinner with the bass, guitar, keyboards, drums, reeds and melodica; Vin Gordon and David Fullwood supply trombone/trumpet. Exact production, hardware, tuning baseline, technique defaults and axes are authored modern choices.","exemplars":["Nat Birchall / Al Breadwinner — Tradition Disc In Dub"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
  "virgin_islands_reggae": {"parent":"bassSystem.reggaeDiaspora","axes":{"harm":0,"pitch":0,"ornament":0,"meter":0,"density":1,"transmission":1,"improv":0,"soundTech":1,"intensity":0,"voice":0,"timbre":0,"percussion":1,"cyclicity":2},"description":"A resonant bass line and measured drum groove support the singer, while guitar and keyboards provide offbeat chords. Horn melodies can add responses and depth to the arrangement. The roots-oriented sound gives particular weight to vocal delivery, rhythmic patience and the relationship between the song and its accompanying groove.","exemplars":["Vaughn Benjamin / Akae Beka — Exalt the Crown"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
  "west_coast_reggae": {"parent":"bassSystem.reggaeDiaspora","axes":{"harm":0,"pitch":0,"ornament":0,"meter":0,"density":1,"transmission":1,"improv":0,"soundTech":1,"intensity":0,"voice":0,"timbre":0,"percussion":1,"cyclicity":2},"description":"Electric guitar, bass, drums and keyboards underpin sung refrains, with brass appearing in some arrangements. Reggae offbeats alternate or combine with more forceful rock accompaniment, while vocal harmonies and accessible hooks give the songs a strong melodic focus. The field is shaped by coastal band scenes and their mixture of reggae groove and amplified rock performance.","exemplars":["Iration — Daytrippin, core-band branch"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
  "vintage_reggae": {"parent":"bassSystem.reggaeDiaspora","axes":{"harm":0,"pitch":0,"ornament":0,"meter":0,"density":1,"transmission":1,"improv":0,"soundTech":1,"intensity":0,"voice":0,"timbre":0,"percussion":1,"cyclicity":2},"description":"The groove centres on bass and drums, with clipped guitar, piano and organ chords creating rhythmic space around the voice. Vocal songs and instrumental versions share the same foundations, with arrangement and mixing bringing different parts forward. Classic recordings such as B. B. Seaton’s work at Treasure Isle illustrate the close connection between musicianship, songwriting and studio production.","exemplars":["B.B. Seaton — Is Life, core rhythm arrangement"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
