  "jawaiian": {"parent":"bassSystem.reggaeDiaspora","axes":{"harm":0,"pitch":0,"ornament":0,"meter":0,"density":0,"transmission":0,"improv":0,"soundTech":0,"intensity":0,"voice":0,"timbre":0,"percussion":0,"cyclicity":0},"description":"Ho’omana, complete advertised three-person Hawaiian/Jawaiian live-trio practice: Manley Bush lead/background voice, six-string slack-key guitar and ukulele; Robert Uncangco lead/background voice and six-string rhythm guitar; Henry Lopez lead/background voice, six-string guitar and electric bass. Agent names Jawaiian reggae in this trio repertoire; artist-managed booking profile supplies all three vocal credits and Henry guitar doubling. This is a full practice union with alternation/doubling, not all instruments simultaneously played by three people. Optional separately booked hula/Tahitian dancers and drummers are not part of the selected trio, and no actual issued album or single is being partially reconstructed. Earlier Eddy Navarro lineup is not conflated with Henry Lopez. Jawaiian identifies the Hawaiian reggae mixture and its island/diaspora community, positively named by the trio agent. Ethnomusicologist Sunaina Keonaona Kale describes its distinct Hawaiian adoption and smoother R&B/pop-inflected leisure-dance practice in her university first-person research interview. It overlaps reggae and Hawaiian popular music; existing Hawaiian slack-key is a guitar technique/repertoire tradition, vintage Hawaiian concerns earlier hapa-haole commercial practice, and Hawaiian hip hop explicitly centers MC delivery while acknowledging Jawaiian influence. This selected diaspora trio also plays other Hawaiian repertoire; not every song in its book is asserted reggae. Source-positive Jawaiian branch uses its whole documented trio palette, not an invented universal genre ensemble. Authored collective live rendition retains three distinct singers with editorial chordal homophonic voicing and live ensemble capture; exact intervals are not measured. One acoustic-guitar owner represents the shared six-string family and positively documented slack-key role plus rhythm-guitar doublings. Authored open-G fingerpicked chordal arrangement is applied coherently to that family; no claim that every source guitar uses this exact tuning or that generic rhythm guitar is a separate omitted instrument. Sitka-spruce/rosewood dreadnought with80/20-bronze strings, concert mahogany strummed ukulele and alder dual-single-coil fingerstyle electric bass are editorial physical choices. Global tuning and optional capture apparatus omitted. No traditional chant physiology, measured original falsetto register or specific original recording chain is assumed.","exemplars":["Ho’omana — complete Henry Lopez-era live trio practice"],"status":"documented","crossRefs":[]},
  "jesus_movement": {"parent":"ritualDevotional.christianGospel","axes":{"harm":0,"pitch":0,"ornament":0,"meter":0,"density":-1,"transmission":0,"improv":0,"soundTech":0,"intensity":0,"voice":2,"timbre":0,"percussion":-1,"cyclicity":0},"description":"Direct melodies and accessible harmonies support expressive singing and group participation. Piano or guitar can carry intimate devotional songs, while fuller rock arrangements bring the same repertoire into concerts and communal gatherings.","exemplars":["Love Song","Keith Green","Larry Norman"],"crossRefs":[]},
